asters, enforce strict safety standards, and phase out dangerous old dams while investing in safer, smaller, and better-sited projects. Many countries are now decommissioning high-risk dams and replacing them with modern, safer designs. That is accountability in action. Finally, I want to acknowledge that your experience may mean no argument I offer will feel adequate. A village lost, a river changed—those are not abstractions. If the global energy transition can be achieved without repeating those mistakes, then we must fight for that. If it cannot, then we must be honest about the trade-offs. But given the urgent need to move away from fossil fuels, I believe hydroelectricity, built and managed responsibly, remains a necessary part of the solution—not because it is perfect, but because the alternatives are often far worse.
